 To solve the equation \(x^4 + 64 = 0\), we start by rewriting it as \(x^4 = -64\). We can express \(-64\) in polar form as \(64 \text{cis} (180^\circ)\) (or \(64 e^{i\pi}\) in exponential form), since \(-64\) has a magnitude of 64 and an argument of \(180^\circ\) (or \(\pi\) radians). The general solution for \(x^4 = 64 \text{cis} (180^\circ)\) is given by \(x = 2 \text{cis} \left(\frac{180^\circ + 360^\circ k}{4}\right)\) for \(k = 0, 1, 2, 3\). This simplifies to \(x = 2 \text{cis} (45^\circ + 90^\circ k)\). So the solutions are: \[
x_1 = 2 \text{cis} (45^\circ) = 2 \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} + i \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = \sqrt{2} + i \sqrt{2}
\] \[
x_2 = 2 \text{cis} (135^\circ) = 2 \left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} + i \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = -\sqrt{2} + i \sqrt{2}
\] \[
x_3 = 2 \text{cis} (225^\circ) = 2 \left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} - i \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = -\sqrt{2} - i \sqrt{2}
\] \[
x_4 = 2 \text{cis} (315^\circ) = 2 \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} - i \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = \sqrt{2} - i \sqrt{2}
\] Therefore, the solutions to the equation \(x^4 + 64 = 0\) are \(\sqrt{2} + i \sqrt{2}, -\sqrt{2} + i \sqrt{2}, -\sqrt{2} - i \sqrt{2}, \sqrt{2} - i \sqrt{2}\). </think> <answer> \sqrt{2} + i \sqrt{2}, -\sqrt{2} + i \sqrt{2}, -\sqrt{2} - i \sqrt{2}, \sqrt{2} - i \sqrt{2} </answer>

